<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cyfraith-hywel">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyfraith Hywel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Laws of Hywel Dda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyfraith Hywel, the law of Hywel, was the native law of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, traditionally said to have been codified under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a> in the 10th century. Surviving in law books in Welsh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> from the 13th century, it set rules on inheritance, compensation for killing and injury, status, marriage and land, and stayed partly in use until the Laws in Wales Acts of the 16th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyfraith-hywel.jpg" alt="Cyfraith Hywel" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Proclaiming_the_Laws_of_Howel_Dda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Welsh law was a form of Celtic law close to the Brehon law of Ireland and to the customs of the Britons of Strathclyde, handed down orally by jurists and bards. Most law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> open with a preamble telling how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel the Good</a>, son of Cadell, summoned six men from every commote to the White House on the Taf in Dyfed and chose twelve laymen and one scholar, Master Blegywryd, to frame the laws. Jurists revised the texts as circumstances changed, while few rulers are known to have done so, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleddyn-ap-cynfyn">Bleddyn ap Cynfyn</a> in Powys among them.</p><p>No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> survives from Hywel's time. The earliest, of the early or mid 13th century, are Peniarth 28, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> but probably translated from Welsh, and Peniarth 29, the Black Book of Chirk, in Welsh. The texts fall into the Cyfnerth, Blegywryd and Iorwerth Redactions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The laws of the court set out the rank and dues of the king, queen, heir and household officers; the laws of the country covered the rest. Society was divided into rulers, free Welsh, serfs, resident foreigners and slaves, and penalties varied with status. Killing was an offence against the family, settled by galanas, blood money paid by the killer's kin and reckoned by the victim's status, after which vengeance was given up; poisoning carried death. The hands, eyes, lips, feet and nose were valued at 480 pennies each.</p><p>Land passed jointly to all the sons, acknowledged illegitimate sons included, and the youngest divided it, a rule at odds with canon law. A woman who eloped without her kindred's consent gained, after seven years, the same entitlements as one given in marriage. Foreigners became native only after three generations in the north. Cases were heard in the courts of the cantref, later of the commote, with professional judges in Gwynedd.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Welsh law became a badge of nationhood in the 12th and 13th centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn the Last</a> argued against Edward I that each province should keep its own laws. The last recorded case under it concerned land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a> in 1540, four years after the 1536 Act had stipulated that only English law be used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, and arbiters still applied its principles informally in the 17th century. William Wotton's translation appeared in 1730, and Aneurin Owen's edition of 1841, Ancient laws and institutions of Wales, first identified the redactions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cyfraith Hywel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hywel was credited with codifying Welsh law, the <b>Cyfraith Hywel</b>, which governed inheritance, compensation and status.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-deheubarth">Kingdom of Deheubarth</a></li></ul></section>
